order-9 Wager witness-validator audit (2026-08-14)

Decision: reclassify wager.frozen_independence, wager.global_protocol_invariant, and
wager.named_models from regression_only to witness_validator.

- Existing zero-argument methods are retained unchanged as regression fixtures.
- Additive overloads validate independently supplied finite candidates and reject malformed
  negative encodings instead of treating encoding failure as a mathematical counterexample.
- The frozen-independence overload validates positive and negative witnesses for W1-W4 and W6.
  This is intentionally broader than its bound declaration ERIEC.Wager.W6_indep.
- The global-invariant overload independently evaluates sentence(positive), !sentence(negative),
  and every supplied pack on both witnesses.  The sentence and packs remain opaque parameters.
- The named-model overload gives KBad, KPlus, M0, MPlus, and MCyc independent carriers and checks
  ConvHolds or finite W6 as appropriate.
- W6 validation uses a non-reflexive transitive closure, justified on finite carriers by
  ERIEC.Wager.W6_iff_dc_transGen_self.

The overloads do not establish that supplied candidates are the fixed Lean objects
consistentImpl, noViableImpl, KBad, KPlus, M0, MPlus, or MCyc, and no checked canonical decoder is
introduced.  Under the shared order-9/order-13 classification rule, these are witness validators,
not exact decisions of the bound closed theorems.
